    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change returns `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively.\n\nTo keep structural impact low, I isolated the new behavior into a small cohesive unit rather than growing the large controller method:\n\n1. **New helper class `Namesakes.java`** (mirrors the existing `Households` helper) \u2014 holds the counting logic in a class with near-zero surrounding complexity, so the logic carries no `WMC_other` tax.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 a single added line: `owner.setNamesakeCount(Namesakes.count(owners, owner));`. It reuses the `owners` collection already fetched for the telephone/household checks, and the method's own cyclomatic complexity is unchanged.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 a `@Transient Integer namesakeCount` field with a trivial getter/setter, following the exact pattern of the existing transient `householdId` (which is likewise computed at create time and survives read-back within the request's persistence context).\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema. The DTO is generated at build time and MapStruct auto-maps the same-named property, so no mapper edit was needed.\n\nThe `coreCountsNamesakes` acceptance test and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
